course: dictionary
lesson: 2382
title: Curd in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, so the sentence around them tells you which one the writer means. «Cheese is made from curds, the soft lumps of thickened milk, pressed and ripened.» The words soft lumps and thickened milk are the clues that tell you what curds are.
explain: Look for a word that is the opposite or partner of the new word. «Cheese-makers add rennet to milk, which separates into solid curds and watery whey.» Solid is set against watery, so curds must be the thick, solid part and whey must be the runny liquid.
explain: Then test your meaning. Swap it into the sentence: «Lemon curd is a thick, creamy spread made with fruit juice, sugar, eggs and butter.» Does a thick, creamy spread fit? Yes, so your meaning works.
fact: Curds are the thick white lumps that form when milk goes sour or is made to separate.
fact: Whey is the watery liquid left behind when curds form.
fact: Lemon curd is a thick, creamy spread made with fruit juice, sugar, eggs and butter.
fact: Curd can also mean soft cheese, like cottage cheese, or bean curd (tofu).
fact: Cheese is made from curds, so curds are the part of milk that becomes solid.
exercise: «Cheese-makers add rennet to milk, which separates into solid curds and watery whey.» What are curds? | A sweet sauce for puddings | A kind of bread | *Solid, thick lumps | The watery liquid left behind
exercise: Little Miss Muffet ate «her curds and whey». Which word names the watery liquid? | *Whey | Cheese | Curds | Lemon curd
exercise: «Lemon curd is a thick, creamy spread made with fruit juice, sugar, eggs and butter.» What does curd mean here? | *A thick, creamy spread | A type of tree | A fizzy drink | Watery milk
test: «Cheese is made from curds, the soft lumps of thickened milk, pressed and ripened.» Which word tells you that curds are soft? | pressed | cheese | ripened | *soft
test: Curd can mean soft cheese, like cottage cheese. Which food does this meaning name? | Rennet | Lemon juice | *Cottage cheese | Butter
exam: In «solid curds and watery whey», why does the writer put solid next to curds and watery next to whey? | To show that both are sweet and sticky | To show that curds are made of water | *To show that the two parts are different: one thick and one runny | To show that whey is thicker than curds
